{"title":"Velisse Skin","sub":"A refill pouch: greener, but will it eat our bottle sales?","type":"Product launch","industry":"Beauty and personal care","geo":"France","diff":2,"fmt":"Interviewer-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Cannibalisation · channel incentives","prompt":"Velisse Skin sells a premium face serum in French pharmacies at $48 a bottle. It plans to launch a refill pouch at $40 that customers pour into their existing bottle. The sales team worries that refills will cannibalise bottle sales. The CEO asks for the full economics.","clarify":[{"q":"Costs?","a":"A bottle costs $18 to make, including $7 of glass and pump. A refill pouch costs $11."},{"q":"Channel?","a":"Pharmacies keep about 35% of the retail price, on bottles and refills alike."},{"q":"Customers?","a":"280,000 regular customers buying about 3 bottles a year."},{"q":"Research?","a":"22% of customers would switch to refills; they'd buy slightly more often (3.4 times a year) because refills are cheaper. 32,000 new, sustainability-minded customers would join."},{"q":"Launch costs?","a":"$1.35M a year for refill displays and marketing."}],"structure":{"root":"Is the refill worth launching?","branches":[{"head":"Switchers","items":["Margin per refill vs per bottle","Purchase frequency"]},{"head":"New customers","items":["Number, purchases per year"]},{"head":"Channel","items":["Pharmacy earnings on refills vs bottles"]},{"head":"Costs","items":["Displays and marketing"]}]},"hypothesis":"Refills sell for less but cost much less to make, so Velisse Skin's margin per sale may be similar. If that's true, cannibalisation is not the real risk.","questions":[{"q":"Compare Velisse Skin's margin on a bottle and a refill. What happens to a customer who switches?","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"393.86pt\" height=\"181.412pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 393.86 181.412\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{strok